\section{Interpolation modes}{

For \code{n} reduced elements and a probability \code{q}, let
\code{h = 1 + (n - 1) * q} be the position \code{q} falls at in the sorted values,
with \code{lo = floor(h)}, \code{hi = ceiling(h)} and \code{frac = h - lo}. Then, writing
\code{sorted} for the reduced values in sorted order:
\itemize{
\item \code{"linear"} (default): \code{(1 - frac) * sorted[lo] + frac * sorted[hi]}.
\item \code{"lower"}: \code{sorted[lo]} — the lower bracket of \code{linear}.
\item \code{"higher"}: \code{sorted[hi]} — the upper bracket of \code{linear}.
\item \code{"nearest"}: \code{sorted[lo]} if \code{frac < 0.5} else \code{sorted[hi]}.
\item \code{"midpoint"}: \code{(sorted[lo] + sorted[hi]) / 2}.
}

Reducing several axes at once ranks all of their elements together, so
\code{nv_quantile(x, q, axes = c(1, 2))} equals
\code{nv_quantile(nv_flatten(x), q)} for a matrix \code{x}.
